course: dictionary
lesson: 3671
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word five can mean different things. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning fits. «The hand has five fingers.» Here five is a number, and you can count the fingers to check.
explain: Five is the number 5, one more than four. When you meet five in a sentence, ask: is it a number, money, or part of a phrase? «She paid with a five» means a five-pound note, because paying is the clue.
explain: Some phrases change the meaning completely. «He gave me a high five» means he slapped his hand against mine to say well done. It is not about counting. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: Five is the number 5, one more than four.
fact: A five can be the digit 5 or a five-pound note.
fact: «High five» means slapping hands together to say well done.
fact: Counting in fives goes 5, 10, 15, 20, and so on.
fact: Context clues help you choose the right meaning of a word with more than one sense.
exercise: «Jesus fed five thousand people with a boy's five loaves and two fish.» How many loaves did the boy have? | Five thousand | Twelve | *Five | Two
exercise: Lily says «give me five» means count to five. What does a high five mean? | A horseshoe-shaped scar | Paying with a five-pound note | Counting five fingers | *Slapping hands together to say well done
exercise: Counting in fives: 5, 10, 15, what comes next? | 14 | 16 | 25 | *20
test: «She paid for the sweets with a five.» What is a five here? | One more than six | Slapping hands to say well done | *A five-pound note | A horseshoe-shaped scar
test: Five is one more than which number? | Ten | *Four | Three | Six
exam: «Give me five!» means what? | *Slap your hand against mine to say well done | Give a horse chestnut leaf to a friend | Pay with a five-pound note | Count from one to five
